WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.520
- This map shows the flu is going crazy.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.747
Levels are really high in
most of the country right now.

00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:07.890
RSV and covid are also circulating,

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.350
setting up a potential tripledemic.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.880
- [Narrator] This season's triple threat

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.180
is taking a toll both
on patients and staff.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.550
- Now the WHO and CDC are worried

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.070
about a potential surge
in the cases of strep

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.750
and some public health experts say

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.970
that the culprit could be the flu.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:24.990
Here's why.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.280
The flu is a virus

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.840
and it can leave you more vulnerable

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.370
to getting bacterial infections,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.110
like with Streptococcus bacteria,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.250
which causes strep.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.110
There are three main reasons for that.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.590
First, flu makes it easier
for bacteria to infect you.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.290
It damages little hairlike
structures called cilia

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.880
that line the respiratory tract.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.350
- The cilia can't clean out,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.780
or wash out the lungs like they're used to

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.090
and the bacteria can get stuck down there

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.090
and then they can replicate
and cause more problems.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.120
- [Daniela] Second, flu
infections also cause inflammation

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.280
that starts to break apart the tight links

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.170
between cells and the lining of our lungs,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.150
which acts as a protective barrier.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.370
- That allow bugs to come through,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.170
almost like a little bug highway.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.300
- [Daniela] Third, the flu
strains the immune system.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.610
So, if strep bacteria
managed to infect you

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.770
the immune system has a
harder time fighting them off.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.180
- Every time there's a flu season

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.250
there's gonna be a lot more incidents

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.770
of strep secondary infections.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.960
- [Daniela] Those can lead to
complications like pneumoniae

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.420
or in very rare cases
the toxic shock syndrome

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.130
that doctors are seeing in kids.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.870
Look at all this red and purple.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.330
This flu season is particularly bad.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.520
That's partly because of the pandemic.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.160
Masking, social distancing
and working remotely

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.720
protected us against covid,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.520
but those precautions also kept us

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.840
from getting sick generally.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.120
- And there isn't a lot
of population immunity

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.590
against many infections.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.720
- [Daniela] So viruses
like RSV and the flu

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.280
can now get around more easily.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.720
The cases of strep are coming

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.270
at an unusual time of
year from what's expected.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.160
It's a trend we're seeing with RSV

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.190
and other infectious diseases
since the lockdowns ended.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.500
Flu cases are spiking really quickly

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.820
and that's what might be leaving us

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.470
more vulnerable to strep.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.230
- There's gonna be so many more cases

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.630
of strep causing serious infection.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.100
- Who is most at risk?

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.920
Well, that depends on the kind of strep.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.270
- The number of children who have died

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.390
from strep A across the UK since September

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.843
has now risen to 19.

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:16.890
- [Daniela] And the CDC is also looking

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.650
at a possible spike in strep
A related complications

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.060
in kids in the US.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.550
- Children, particularly smaller children,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.700
have all kinds of
different strains of strep

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.500
in their nose and throat every single day

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.060
that never causes a problem.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.580
- Their immune system can
typically fight them off,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.410
but things are more complicated right now

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.650
thanks to surges in flu and
lower level population immunity.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.740
Plus, it's possible that
strep A could be changing too,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.600
to become more invasive.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.790
All that together is
why we might be seeing

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.520
a potential increase in
serious strep A cases

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.350
and why it's getting a lot of attention.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.910
But it's a different family of strep

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.830
that typically causes the most problems.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.350
It's called Streptococcus pneumoniae.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.720
- The vast majority of flu seasons

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.750
influenza isn't actually
the source of death.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.480
It's the infection caused
by the strep pneumoniae

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.400
and the pneumoniae associated with that.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.920
- Doctors recommend
that people 65 and over

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.660
get a vaccine against strep pneumoniae

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.400
to prevent complications from flu.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.960
They say the same is true for people

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.340
with compromised immune systems,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.470
heart disease or chronic lung conditions.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.760
But for most people

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.100
strep doesn't cause
life-threatening issues.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.800
- It's really rare to
have bad consequences

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.660
from strep as opposed to influenza,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.440
which kills 35,000 to
70,000 people a year.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.020
- That's why Peter and David
said it was really important

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.750
to get our flu shots,
especially this year.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.370
And right now vaccination rates

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.080
for flu are pretty low.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.940
So, how do we prevent all this?

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:39.773
It's simple.

00:03:39.773 --> 00:03:42.810
Wash your hands, wear a
mask, and get your flu shot.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.150
And if you do end up
testing positive for strep,

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.040
antibiotics work really well.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.810
Remember, regardless of what you have,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.580
stay home so you don't pass it on.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.130
Infections aren't mutually exclusive.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.740
Just because you just got over one illness

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.040
doesn't mean you're immune to others.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.207
(gentle violin music)

